@title
  utterance:
    text: Welcome to slideSonnet. Everything you're about to hear was written as plain text in a sidecar file, and rendered straight from a PDF. Let me show you how.
  pause: 0.5

@intro-q
  utterance:
    voice: alex
    text: So what does slideSonnet actually do?

@intro-a
  utterance:
    text: It turns your slide deck into a narrated lecture video. Voices, page timing, subtitles. All driven by one plain text file.

@why-q
  utterance:
    voice: alex
    text: Why not just put the narration inside the slides themselves?

@why-a
  utterance:
    text: Because the PDF is your frozen artifact. Keeping narration in a separate, git-diffable file means you can rewrite a line and re-render in seconds. No recompiling the slides, no re-recording a microphone.

@markers
  utterance:
    text: Step one: mark your slides. Add the slideSonnet package to your Beamer source, and give every slide a stable id with the ess-ess-eye-dee macro. The id gets stamped invisibly onto each page, so narration binds to the id, not to a page number that shifts when you insert a slide.

@init
  utterance:
    text: Step two: scaffold the sidecar. Run slidesonnet init on your PDF, and it reads the ids straight out of the document and writes a blank narration file, one block per slide, ready for you to fill in. By hand, or by an LLM.

@format
  utterance:
    text: Each slide id gets a block of utterances and pauses.
  utterance:
    voice: alex
    text: Wait. Is that the block we're hearing right now?
  utterance:
    pace: slow
    text: Yes. Word for word.

@edit-q
  utterance:
    voice: alex
    text: Do I really have to write that text file by hand?

@edit-a
  utterance:
    text: You can, or you can run slidesonnet edit for a local editor. Page through the deck, type narration beside each slide, and preview the whole thing. The preview bakes in your pauses, so it plays back exactly like the final video will.

@export
  utterance:
    text: Step four: render. Run slidesonnet export with the kokoro engine for free, natural local speech, or a cloud engine for studio voices. Want a fast rough cut? Use silent mode, or estimate timing, to skip speech entirely. Subtitles are written alongside the video automatically.

@cache-q
  utterance:
    voice: alex
    text: Doesn't re-rendering everything get slow?

@cache-a
  utterance:
    text: Audio is cached by content. Edit one block, and only that line is re-synthesized. Everything else is reused. And switching engines never throws away audio you already paid for.

@recap
  utterance:
    text: That's the whole workflow. Mark your slides, scaffold the narration, check that the ids line up, write and preview, then export. Every step is scriptable, so an LLM or a Makefile can drive the entire pipeline without ever opening the editor.

@closing
  utterance:
    text: Mark your slides. Write narration. Render. Give slideSonnet a try.
  pause: 1
